\chapter{Finite recursive ownership}
\label{ch:26}
\index{Finite recursive ownership}
\index{Ownership!unique}
\index{Cleanup!iterative destruction}

A recursive declaration needs indirection to have a finite layout. The implemented C11 slice admits recursive edges through \OnxCodeTerm{own<Named>}. The \OnxCodeTerm{box} operation creates a unique owner. Transfer uses explicit \OnxCodeTerm{move}, and consuming \OnxCodeTerm{take} and \OnxCodeTerm{drop} have distinct lifecycle effects. A moved binding no longer holds a usable owner. The admitted set excludes unrestricted pointer graphs and shared cycles.

\section{Build a two-element list}
A \OnxCodeTerm{Cons} holds a number and owns the rest of the list.
If its second field held another \OnxCodeTerm{List} by value, calculating
the size of one node would require knowing the size of that node already.
An owning field has a fixed representation regardless of how many nodes
follow it. Even the empty tail has a box in this example: its payload
is the \OnxCodeTerm{Nil} variant.

\OnxInputCode[firstline=1,lastline=9]{examples/consume-list.on1x}

Start with \OnxCodeTerm{empty()}, prepend 5, then prepend 8. There are
now three boxes, but only one owner in the caller: the root of
$8 \to 5 \to \mathrm{Nil}$. At each prepend the caller moves its old
root into the parameter, and the callee moves that parameter into the
new node. Neither move allocates or copies a tail. The call to
\OnxCodeTerm{box} allocates just the new head.

\section{Walk the list without keeping it}
\OnxInputCode[firstline=11,lastline=24]{examples/consume-list.on1x}

At the top of each iteration, \OnxCodeTerm{cursor} owns the entire
unread suffix. Taking it frees one box and puts the payload in
\OnxCodeTerm{node}. The consuming match now has two obligations. In
the \OnxCodeTerm{Cons} arm, it gives the tail to the arm, which must
restore the cursor before going around again. In the \OnxCodeTerm{Nil}
arm there is nothing to restore: the loop returns its sum. For our list
the successive totals at the top of the loop are 0, 8, and 13. On the
last iteration \OnxCodeTerm{take} frees the empty tail's box too.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
python3 on1x/tools/on1x-build.py on1x/book/examples/consume-list.on1x \
  --on1xc build/on1x/on1xc -o build/consume-list
build/consume-list
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

The program checks the empty case and prints 13 for the two-element
case. What if the caller wants to discard the list instead? It can
call \OnxCodeTerm{drop(list)} in place of the final print. That consumes
the same root without computing a sum; the runtime queues child boxes
for destruction rather than recursing down the C stack. In either
version the caller cannot use \OnxCodeTerm{list} afterward.

Try changing the call to pass \OnxCodeTerm{list} without its move.
The parameter owns its argument; merely naming the caller's root cannot
give the callee a second copy of it. This change should fail during
checking, before the first iteration of the traversal can run.

